$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 1}\dfrac{x\ln(x)}{x^2-1}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 1}\dfrac{\dfrac{d}{dx}[x\ln(x)]}{\dfrac{d}{dx}[x^2-1]}\qquad\gray{\text{L’Hôpital’s rule}}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 1}\dfrac{\ln(x)+x\cdot\dfrac1x}{2x}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{\ln(1)+1}{2(1)}\qquad\gray{\text{Substitution}}
\\\\
&=\dfrac12
\end{align}$

Note that we were only able to use L’Hôpital’s rule because the limit $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 1}\dfrac{\dfrac{d}{dx}[x\ln(x)]}{\dfrac{d}{dx}[x^2-1]}$ actually exists.
